* economist.scheme
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* dash translation of Stata's economist scheme.
*
* PROVENANCE
*   Source Stata scheme : /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-economist.scheme
*                         (Stata #include chain: s2color -> economist)
*   Style files         : /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/style/*.style
*   Stata version       : StataNow 19.5 (MP), born 12 Aug 2026
*   Extracted           : 2026-09-19 (fixture generated_utc 2026-09-19T16:13:55Z)
*   Derived from        : test/fixtures/scheme_truth.json, entry "economist",
*                         generated by scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py from
*                         Stata's own scheme-*.scheme and style/*.style files.
*   Last known true as of the extraction date above.  Re-run the extractor and
*   re-diff this file after any Stata upgrade; the header is only true for the
*   version and date recorded in it.
*
* WHY EVERY COLOUR IS WRITTEN AS RESOLVED #rrggbb HEX
*   OptionTranslator._COLOR_MAP knows only 72 Stata colour names.
*   SchemeParser._color() (dash_render.py:863) swallows a token it cannot
*   resolve and returns its "#000000" default (dash_render.py:934), so a
*   name-based translation renders unknown series black.  Hex is
*   self-contained and immune to that.
*
* SELF-CONTAINED BY DESIGN
*   Stata's scheme-economist.scheme #includes scheme-s2color.scheme.  This file
*   deliberately does NOT "#include s2color": dash's schemes/s2color.scheme
*   would silently contribute values for any key omitted here (its graphsize is
*   5.5x4, economist's is 2.2x2.2), which is exactly the kind of invisible
*   inheritance this translation exists to remove.  Every value below is stated
*   outright, already resolved through Stata's p#stub -> pstub -> p# -> p chain.
*
* Property categories used by dash SchemeParser:
*   color, symbol, linepattern, linewidth, intensity, gsize, graphsize,
*   symbolsize, clockdir, numstyle, yesno
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

label "Economist"

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Background and region colors
* economist paints the whole canvas, the plot region and the legend in the same
* pale blue; the legend border is that same colour, i.e. invisible by design.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
color background      #c6d3df
color plotregion      #c6d3df
color legend          #c6d3df
color legend_line     #c6d3df
color major_grid      #ffffff
color minor_grid      #ffffff
color heading         #000000
color axisline        #000000

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Per-plot color cycle (p1-p15), resolved from Stata's color-*.style files
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
color p1      #3e647d
color p2      #7b92a8
color p3      #82c0e9
color p4      #2d6d66
color p5      #bfa19c
color p6      #008bbc
color p7      #97b6b0
color p8      #d7d29e
color p9      #1a476f
color p10     #90353b
color p11     #9c8847
color p12     #938dd2
color p13     #6e8e84
color p14     #c10534
color p15     #cac27e

* Per-plot line color (for line/connected plots).
* economist sets no "color p#line"; Stata resolves these through the documented
* p#stub -> pstub -> p# -> p chain, landing on "color p#".  Spelled out here
* because SchemeParser reads p#line by exact key with no fallback.
color p1line   #3e647d
color p2line   #7b92a8
color p3line   #82c0e9
color p4line   #2d6d66
color p5line   #bfa19c
color p6line   #008bbc
color p7line   #97b6b0
color p8line   #d7d29e
color p9line   #1a476f
color p10line  #90353b
color p11line  #9c8847
color p12line  #938dd2
color p13line  #6e8e84
color p14line  #c10534
color p15line  #cac27e

* Per-plot bar/area fill color (resolves to "color p#", as above)
color p1bar    #3e647d
color p2bar    #7b92a8
color p3bar    #82c0e9
color p4bar    #2d6d66
color p5bar    #bfa19c
color p6bar    #008bbc
color p7bar    #97b6b0
color p8bar    #d7d29e
color p9bar    #1a476f
color p10bar   #90353b
color p11bar   #9c8847
color p12bar   #938dd2
color p13bar   #6e8e84
color p14bar   #c10534
color p15bar   #cac27e

* Per-plot marker fill color (resolves to "color p#", as above)
color p1mark   #3e647d
color p2mark   #7b92a8
color p3mark   #82c0e9
color p4mark   #2d6d66
color p5mark   #bfa19c
color p6mark   #008bbc
color p7mark   #97b6b0
color p8mark   #d7d29e
color p9mark   #1a476f
color p10mark  #90353b
color p11mark  #9c8847
color p12mark  #938dd2
color p13mark  #6e8e84
color p14mark  #c10534
color p15mark  #cac27e

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Marker symbol cycle
* economist inherits s2color's single "symbol p circle" class default: every
* plot in the cycle is a circle.  Written out p1-p15 because SchemeParser has
* no fallback.  The earlier approximation cycled 15 different symbols; Stata
* does not, so that variation is removed here.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbol p1     circle
symbol p2     circle
symbol p3     circle
symbol p4     circle
symbol p5     circle
symbol p6     circle
symbol p7     circle
symbol p8     circle
symbol p9     circle
symbol p10    circle
symbol p11    circle
symbol p12    circle
symbol p13    circle
symbol p14    circle
symbol p15    circle

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line patterns for line/connected plots
* A single "linepattern p solid" class default: economist distinguishes series
* by colour, never by dash pattern.  The earlier approximation cycled dash,
* longdash, dot and friends; Stata does not.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
linepattern p1line   solid
linepattern p2line   solid
linepattern p3line   solid
linepattern p4line   solid
linepattern p5line   solid
linepattern p6line   solid
linepattern p7line   solid
linepattern p8line   solid
linepattern p9line   solid
linepattern p10line  solid
linepattern p11line  solid
linepattern p12line  solid
linepattern p13line  solid
linepattern p14line  solid
linepattern p15line  solid

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line widths.  economist declares its plot line width as the bare number 1.2
* (Stata linewidth units), not as a style name; recorded exactly as declared.
* The grid width is the "medium" style (.30 Stata units).
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
linewidth p           1.2
linewidth major_grid  medium

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Fill intensities.
* "intensity area" is not set by any Stata base scheme, economist included; it
* resolves through the bare "intensity" class default, which is full here.
* The resolved value is recorded, not guessed.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
intensity p        inten80
intensity bar      full
intensity area     full

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Font sizes (Stata gsize keywords, as declared).
* economist gives its heading the bare relative size 6.0 rather than a style
* name; recorded as declared.  SchemeParser._gsize() converts a numeric gsize
* arithmetically, so it needs the number, not a name substituted for it.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gsize text          medium
gsize heading       6.0
gsize axis_title    medium
gsize tick_label    medium
gsize note          large

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Graph dimensions, in inches, as Stata declares them (consumed by section 10
* for figure proportions).  economist is square and small: 2.2 x 2.2.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
graphsize x  2.2
graphsize y  2.2

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Default marker size for the p cycle, and legend placement.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbolsize p              large
clockdir legend_position  12
numstyle legend_cols      3

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Major grid switches.  SchemeParser stores these in _props but
* _init_properties() exposes no attribute for them yet; recorded so the
* declared value is available when a consumer lands, rather than being lost.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* GRID VISIBILITY.  A Stata grid belongs to an AXIS and the key is named for
* that axis's orientation, not for the direction of the lines:
*   style/axisstyle-horizontal_default.style:18  draw_major_grid <- draw_major_hgrid
*   style/axisstyle-vertical_default.style:18    draw_major_grid <- draw_major_vgrid
* The horizontal axis is x, and its major grid lines are the VERTICAL ones at
* the x ticks; the vertical axis is y, and its lines are HORIZONTAL.
* `yesno draw_major_grid' below is transcribed faithfully from Stata's file but
* is DEAD at scheme level: grep "scheme draw_major_grid" over
* /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/style/ returns nothing.  dash used to read it
* for the y axis, which is why every scheme reported "no grid" and dash drew
* both anyway.
yesno draw_major_hgrid  no
yesno draw_major_vgrid yes
yesno draw_major_grid   no

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Added 22sep2026 by scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py (WP-16): directives
* Stata consumes that the translation above did not carry.  Values are
* resolved from the same Stata #include chain and style files as the
* rest of this file (chain: s2color -> economist); colours are
* pre-resolved #rrggbb.  Re-running the script re-checks every directive
* in this file and appends only what is missing.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* Minor grid switches -- EFFECTIVE per-axis values.  twoway builds the x
* axis with `axisstyle horizontal_default' and the y axis with `axisstyle
* vertical_default'; the scheme maps each role to a style name, and that
* style file names the `yesno' key Stata actually reads:
*   x axis: axisstyle horizontal_default = horizontal_default
*           -> style/axisstyle-horizontal_default.style
*           -> draw_major_hgrid / draw_minor_hgrid
*   y axis: axisstyle vertical_default = vertical_nolinetick
*           -> style/axisstyle-vertical_nolinetick.style
*           -> draw_major_nlt_vgrid / draw_minor_nlt_vgrid
* The draw_major_hgrid / draw_major_vgrid lines above hold the same
* effective values.  `yesno draw_minor_grid' is transcribed from Stata's
* file but, like draw_major_grid, no style file reads it.
yesno draw_minor_hgrid  no
yesno draw_minor_vgrid  no
yesno draw_minor_grid   no

* Text colours, one per textbox style: style/textboxstyle-<name>.style
* reads `color <name>'.  title() is heading (above), subtitle()
* subheading, caption() body, note() small_body, legend keys key_label,
* axis titles axis_title, tick labels tick_label.  `color text' and
* `color foreground' are the scheme's general text and foreground
* colours.
color subheading  #000000
color body        #000000
color small_body  #000000
color text        #000000
color axis_title  #000000
color tick_label  #000000
color key_label   #000000
color foreground  #000000

* Text sizes, one per textbox style (Stata gsize style names; the
* fixture's _meta.units gives each name's relative value and its points
* at Stata's 4-inch reference).  note() is drawn with small_body: the
* `gsize note' line above is not the key Stata's note textbox reads.
gsize subheading  medium
gsize body        3.0
gsize small_body  small
gsize key_label   medium

* Minor grid and axis line widths (Stata linewidth style names).
linewidth minor_grid  thin
linewidth axisline    thin

* Legend rows; 0 means not fixed, so the column count above decides.
numstyle legend_rows  0

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* HONEST GAPS
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Nothing in the fixture's "economist" entry is null and its "unresolvable"
* list is empty, so no value in this file is a guess.
*
* Keys carried by the earlier approximation and dropped by the first
* translation because SchemeParser._init_properties() read none of them:
*   color foreground, linewidth minor_grid, gsize subheading, gsize body
* are Stata directives and are carried again in the block added 22sep2026
* above, with the values Stata's own chain resolves to.  `color grid' is
* still not written: economist's grid colour is recorded above under the
* key dash actually reads, "color major_grid".

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* HONEST GAP (project rule 2) -- the p#lineplot keys
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Stata does not read a plain `twoway line` plot's line from the
* `p#line` scheme keys.  It reaches the `p#lineplot` keys through two
* style files:
*   style/seriesstyle-p#line.style:5   set line    scheme p#line
*   style/linestyle-p#line.style:4-7   set width   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set color   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set pattern scheme p#lineplot
* So `linepattern p#lineplot` is the dash pattern of `twoway line`, while
* the `linepattern p#line` block above is what style/linestyle-p#.style
* reads -- the connecting line of `twoway connected`.  Stata documents the
* three keys in scheme_line_plots.sthlp:29-34 as the colour, thickness and
* pattern "for #th line plot".
*
* NO `color p#lineplot` OR `linepattern p#lineplot` DIRECTIVE IS WRITTEN IN
* THIS FILE, because economist's #include chain (s2color -> economist)
* declares none at any level. Of all the scheme files Stata ships, only
* scheme-s2mono.scheme and scheme-s2gmanual.scheme contain a `lineplot`
* directive at all; the three occurrences in scheme-s2color.scheme (lines
* 457, 604 and 1250) are commented-out examples, not settings. There is
* therefore nothing to translate here, and a value written in would be a
* fabrication.
*
* Recorded for the reader, NOT as directives, because these are values Stata
* falls back to rather than values economist writes
* (scheme_line_plots.sthlp:52-56: "If a scheme does not contain some of the
* line-plot specific entries above, then the look of those elements of line
* plots will be determined by default entries that are shared among all
* plottypes"; the shared entries are `color p#` and `linepattern p`, per
* scheme_shared_plots.sthlp:20-31):
*   linepattern p#lineplot -> linepattern p = solid
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-s2color.scheme:599)
*   color       p#lineplot -> color p#, i.e. the `color p1`..`color p15`
*       values already written above
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-economist.scheme:176-190)
